notes=['Recovered exponential running-time hardness of computing randomized description complexity and matched the published 2022 exact-threshold lower bound.','Defined randomized program cost, confidence threshold, unary decision threshold, uniform bounded-error time and input-size scale.','Separated the 2019 probability-gap notation from the 2022 exact language and logarithmic-value-gap promise problem.','Recorded the crucial all-input bounded-error caveat of the stronger gap theorem, corroborated by the February 2026 preprint.','Marked the general exact-decision lower-bound question resolved without claiming a stronger promise-algorithm lower bound.']
save(i,dict(title='Exponential lower bounds for deciding rKt complexity',formal='Historical question, answered affirmatively for exact decision: does deciding randomized time-bounded Kolmogorov complexity require exponential time even for randomized algorithms? Fix a standard efficiently universal randomized Turing machine U, define rKt_U(x) below, and let L_U={(x,1ˢ): rKt_U(x)≤s}. Hirahara’s Theorem 5.3 establishes that there is a constant ε>0 such that L_U∉BPTIME(2^{εN}), where N is the encoded input length and ε may depend on the fixed machine conventions. Thus no uniform randomized algorithm within that worst-case time bound decides every input correctly with probability at least 2/3. The source’s broader question has an exponential lower-bound answer; stronger variants that allow arbitrary behavior inside a promise gap must be distinguished.',definitions='The fixed universal machine U takes a binary program d, an auxiliary string y, and a tape of independent uniformly random bits. It simulates encoded machines with polynomial overhead; the machine and program encoding are fixed independently of every input. For λ∈(0,1], rKt_{U,λ}(x|y) is the minimum of |d|+⌈log₂t⌉ over finite binary programs d and integers t≥1 such that U(d,y) outputs exactly x and halts within t steps with probability at least λ. Failure to halt in time counts as failure. The probability is over the first t random tape bits, which suffice for a t-step computation. No advice, oracle or input x is supplied to the decoding machine. Write rKt_U(x)=rKt_{U,2/3}(x|empty string). The decision input gives x explicitly and a nonnegative integer s in unary, separated by a fixed delimiter; its length N=|x|+s+O(1). The unary convention avoids hiding a very large time threshold in a short integer. BPTIME(T(N)) consists of languages decided by one uniform randomized Turing machine that halts within O(T(N)) steps on every input and random tape, and has error at most 1/3 on every input. The ε lower bound is an existential constant-rate exclusion; it is not a claim that every input requires exponential time. Standard machine and encoding choices change constants. The exact threshold language has no promise gap. In the later logarithmic-gap variant, yes-instances satisfy rKt_U(x)≤s and no-instances satisfy rKt_U(x)>s+C log₂|x| for a sufficiently large fixed C; the intermediate range is outside the correctness promise. In the 2019 source another variant instead separates descriptions succeeding with probabilities β and α, using β=3/4, α=2/3 and threshold |x|/2 in its default notation. These variants must not be identified solely because they are all called MrKtP.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='An unconditional exclusion L_U∉BPTIME(2^{εN}) for some ε>0 settles the exact-decision exponential-lower-bound question. This is the published 2022 Theorem 5.3. Lower bounds on the number of bits needed to describe some strings, or hardness under an unproved assumption, would not by themselves establish it. The separate stronger claim against every promise-respecting randomized gap tester is not certified as solved here.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The 2019 baseline was much weaker',text='The source proves hardness beyond randomized quasipolynomial time for rKt decision and approximation variants, then asks for an exponential lower bound. Its description-length-plus-log-time measure accounts for random decoding.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The exact threshold question has a published answer',text='Hirahara defines the total unary-threshold language in Section 5 and proves its constant-rate exponential randomized time lower bound as Theorem 5.3. The introduction explicitly compares the new exponential scale with Oliveira’s previous quasipolynomial result.',citation='solution'),dict(title='A crucial limitation of the gap theorem',text='Theorems 1.4 and 5.8 additionally rule out infinitely-often fast bounded-error language algorithms consistent with the logarithmic-gap promises. Such an algorithm must have a bounded-error acceptance decision on every input, including the intermediate gap. The paper explicitly distinguishes this from a general promise algorithm, which may have acceptance probability near 1/2 inside the gap.',citation='solution'),dict(title='Recent work preserves that distinction',text='The February 2026 preprint explains why Hirahara’s BPTIME lower bound cannot replace a prBPTIME lower bound in its application. It also gives a different theorem for conditional rKt with a potentially longer auxiliary string; that is not a resolution of the remaining unconditional gap-tester variant.',citation='subsequent')],why='Establishes unconditional exponential computational hardness for recognizing a concrete measure of information content, even when the deciding algorithm can use randomness.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2019',text='Oliveira proves quasipolynomial randomized-time hardness and asks for an exponential lower bound.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2022',text='CCC Theorem 5.3 proves exponential hardness of exact rKt threshold decision. The stronger gap theorem has an explicit all-input bounded-error caveat.',citation='solution'),dict(date='2026-02-16',text='A subsequent preprint reiterates that caveat and studies a different conditional-complexity lower bound.',citation='subsequent')],status='resolved',status_note='Matched the general 2019 exponential-hardness question to Hirahara’s published CCC 2022 Theorem 5.3 for exact decision. Read the machine definition, threshold encoding and the gap theorem’s qualification, and checked the February 2026 follow-up. Resolution is recorded for exact decision; no claim is made that the stronger promise-algorithm variant or the 2019 fixed probability-gap formulation has thereby been settled. No independent proof audit.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Resolved by the exact-threshold theorem, with the materially different BPTIME and promise-BPTIME gap guarantees kept explicit.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='An unconditional strong lower bound for a concrete randomized information-complexity decision problem. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['Randomized Kolmogorov complexity measures the length of a program that prints a string reliably, plus the logarithm of its running time.','The historical question asks whether deciding a threshold for that quantity needs exponential time.','Hirahara’s published 2022 theorem gives an unconditional exponential lower bound for exact threshold decision, even with randomized deciding algorithms.','The accompanying approximation theorem imposes a bounded-error condition on all inputs, including those outside the approximation promise.','Later work explicitly preserves this distinction, so the exact-decision resolution does not certify every stronger promise variant.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','solution','subsequent'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2025/215/revision/1/'])
